# Upload manifest for `gcloud run deploy --source .` (deploy:ssr).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# --------------------
# Without a .gcloudignore, gcloud falls back to .gitignore. That had two effects,
# one merely wasteful and one actively misleading:
#
#  1. Every dirty tracked file and every untracked non-ignored file in the working
#     tree was uploaded. The deploy artifact was therefore derived from whatever
#     happened to be on disk, not from any commit — so the reviewed code and the
#     shipped bytes were unrelated. `deploy:ssr` now refuses to run on a dirty
#     tree (scripts/ops/require-clean-tree.sh); this file bounds what ships even
#     when that gate is deliberately overridden.
#
#  2. `.gitignore` un-ignores `.env.production` (`!.env.production`), which is
#     REQUIRED — it carries the public Firebase web config that the client bundle
#     must bake. That un-ignore is preserved explicitly below. Do not "tidy" it
#     away: dropping it produces a build with no Firebase config and auth dies.
#
# `.env` (real secrets, e.g. VITE_GEMINI_API_KEY) stays excluded. It is a
# build-time Vite variable, so baking it would publish the key in client JS;
# server-side key handling is the correct fix, not uploading this file.

.git
.gitignore
node_modules

# Build outputs — the container rebuilds these from source.
.svelte-kit
build
build-ssr
.output
.vercel
.netlify
.wrangler
vetcpd-dist
ssr-public

# Local tooling / caches
.firebase
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.log
coverage
.vscode
.idea

# Tests and local-only config: not needed at runtime, and keeping them out makes
# the uploaded source match what actually runs.
**/*.test.ts
vitest.config.ts
vitest.standalone.config.ts

# Secrets. Never upload.
.env
.env.*

# ...except the public Firebase web config, which the client build requires.
!.env.production
